@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+# Test bed
+
+The LLM is **simulated**: assistant replies are made up by the driver rather than
+generated. Every run is deterministic and the history exactly controlled — what is under
+test is the template and the tokenizer, not the model.
+
+```bash
+MODEL=/path/to/model.gguf ./run.sh                          # the model's own template
+MODEL=/path/to/model.gguf ./run.sh templates/last.jinja tl  # a bundled template
+```
+
+`LLAMA_SERVER`, `PORT`, `CTX`, `CASES` and `PRE` (a file sourced for backend environment)
+override the defaults. A fresh server per case is deliberate: the probe's learned state is
+per slot and would otherwise carry from one case into the next. The server runs with **two
+slots**: with one, a continuation the picker rejects lands in the same slot anyway and looks
+like a hit. Only an empty second slot makes "nothing reachable" visible — a follow-up served
+from it shows `hit full`, `lcp 0`, and its recomputation is counted as the whole divergence.
+
+## What it checks
+
+Two levels, and the token level is the one that matters:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **text** | what the template renders, whether markers vanish without a trace, and whether a harness *with* the hint channel renders byte-identically to one without |
+| **token** | where two consecutive prompts actually part — computed independently of the server via `/apply-template` + `/tokenize`, then held against what the probe predicted |
+
+Per follow-up turn:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **A reachability** | `restore_at == lcp` — the turn resumes without recomputing anything, by whatever mechanism |
+| **B prediction** | the real divergence is among the candidates the probe considered |
+| **C convergence** | from the third follow-up on, exactly one checkpoint is placed |
+| **D giving up** | a harness the template cannot predict silences the probe after two misses |
+| **E text** | render and tokens are identical with and without the hint channel |
+
+## Harness types
+
+How a client sends its history back is not something a request declares, so the bed walks
+the space:
+
+`preserve` · `strip` · `last-only` · `wrapped` (whole history in one message) ·
+`tools` · `tools-think` · `seam` (prompt end gets re-tokenized as it grows) ·
+`rewrite` (summarises its own history) · `mixed` (switches policy mid-conversation) ·
+`wrapped-hint` (`wrapped`, but declaring where it resumes) ·
+`toolloop` (a **real** tool loop: nine tool results, each its own request, then a user turn) ·
+`mid-system` (a system message before every user turn — needs a template that renders one
+there; the model's own may refuse it)
+
+`toolloop` is the only shape that asks the server to continue after a tool result. Under a
+template that keeps reasoning inside a tool loop and strips it once a user turn follows
+(`lastquery.jinja`), the user turn parts at the *first* think block of the loop — a position
+the probe computes on every iteration and that lies behind the prefill each time. The loop
+is longer than `--ctx-checkpoints` on purpose: the position has to survive the cap.
+
+`wrapped` and `rewrite` are unpredictable by construction — what is asked of the probe
+there is not accuracy but **giving up**, and the hint channel is the remedy. The pair
+`wrapped` / `wrapped-hint` is the case for the channel: same harness, one line of
+difference.
+
+## Templates
+
+`native` (the model's own) plus six in `templates/` that treat reasoning in history
+differently: `preserve`, `strip`, `last`, `plain`, `lastquery` — Qwen3-style: reasoning
+is rendered only for assistant turns after the last user message, so a tool loop keeps it and
+the next user turn strips all of it at once — and `mistral-like`, which attaches the system
+prompt to the **last** user turn and thereby rewrites the previous one every turn. That is
+the one shape a fixed seam offset cannot capture; the probe detects it once per template and
+adds the last user turn as a candidate range.
+
+## The hint channel
+
+`hints-test.py` drives markers end to end against a running server with `--snapshot-path`,
+`--ctx-checkpoints 3` and two slots: a `ckpt` far before the prompt end must produce its
+checkpoint despite the mid-prompt rule; one behind the prefill must be rolled back to within
+budget; markers carried along in history must be **historic** — kept if present, never rolled
+back to, evicted before the current declaration; `ckpt at=agent` must not become a file;
+`snap at=user-1` must land where it says; a `ckpt` in a system message that arrives
+mid-conversation counts as a live hint (the leading system block alone is off limits).
+
+```bash
+BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8099 python3 hints-test.py <telemetry.jsonl>
+```
+
+## The prefix index
+
+`index-test.py` covers the cross-session side separately — that a single session cannot
+inflate the index, that identical preambles do nothing, that one fork earns a file, and
+that a later session loads it:
+
+```bash
+BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8099 python3 index-test.py <telemetry.jsonl> <snapshot-dir>
+```
